[Event "Capablanca plans: Develop with a pin"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2np1n2/1B2p3/1b2P3/2NP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1_w_-_-_0_7"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/KJDr6hau"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2np1n2/1B2p3/1b2P3/2NP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 7"]
[ChapterId "S01"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Development"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 17, move 7"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ Capablanca plans. No tricks today: we collect the small, permanent gains that decide a game. First one — finish your development with a pin. [%cal Yc1g5] } 7. Bg5 { Your last sleeping piece comes out, and it arrives pinning his knight to his queen. One move, two gains. [%tail] [%cal Gc1g5] } (7. Ne2 { The knight steps aside so the c pawn can come later. A real idea — but the bishop on c1 is still the one piece of yours that has never moved. } 7... Ne7 { His knight strolls over to e7 for free. Nothing is lost, and nothing is won: your bishop is still sitting at home. [%cal Gc1g5] }) (7. Bxc6 { You trade bishop for knight and damage his pawns. That is a gain — it just hands him the two bishops, and c1 is still asleep. } 7... bxc6 { His pawns are doubled, so you did collect something. But he has two bishops to your one, and yours has still not found a square. [%cal Gc1g5] }) (7. Ba4 { The bishop saves itself from a trade nobody offered. It is your other bishop, the one on c1, that needs a job. } 7... Bxc3 { His bishop grabs the knight on c3, and taking back with the b pawn doubles your own. The pin on g5 kept everything tidy instead. [%cal Gc1g5] }) (7. h3 { A tidy little move that stops his bishop pinning your knight. It collects nothing, and c1 is still the square with a bishop stuck on it. } 7... Ne7 { He improves a knight while you wait. Bishop to g5 does two jobs with one move: out of the corner, and onto his knight. [%cal Gc1g5] }) 7... Ne7 8. Bxf6 8... gxf6 9. d4 { Your pawn claims the center too. His king sits behind broken pawns on f6, f7 and h7, and the g column in front of it is wide open. [%cal Gd2d4] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Claim room in the center"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/3p4/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_3"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/la0vFFtA"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/3p4/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 3"]
[ChapterId "S02"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Development"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 18, move 3"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ Black has played quietly. Can you claim room for your pieces? } 3. d4 { Your two central pawns give your pieces room and challenge his e pawn. [%tail] [%cal Gd2d4] } (3. Bc4 { The bishop takes a good diagonal and eyes f7. A fine developing move — it just asks his center nothing at all. } 3... Nf6 { He develops and hits your e pawn straight back. Your pawns are still on one row, and his pawn on e5 is still comfortable. [%cal Gd2d4] }) (3. Nc3 { The knight guards e4 before you touch the pawns. Solid — and it leaves his pawn sitting in the middle, unchallenged. [%cal Gc3e4] } 3... c5 { His pawn on c5 now guards d4, and the roomy two-pawn center you were one move away from never happens. [%cal Gd2d4] }) (3. a4 { A pawn on the edge of the board. It gains a little space out there, a long way from where the game is decided. } 3... Nf6 { He develops toward the middle while your pawn walks along the rim. The d pawn is the one that wins your pieces room. [%cal Gd2d4] }) (3. h3 { You stop a bishop pinning your knight. Nothing wrong with it, except that nobody was pinning anything yet. } 3... c5 { He takes the queen-side space and covers d4 into the bargain. The pawn break you were one move from is gone. [%cal Gd2d4] }) 3... exd4 4. Nxd4 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 { Your other knight defends e4 and joins the fight. The e pawn still stands, and your pieces already have all the room they claimed. [%cal Gb1c3] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Castle away from the prepared attack"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3k2r/pbpn1p1p/1p2pp2/5q2/2PP4/5N2/P1PQBPPP/R3K2R_w_KQkq_-_1_12"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/PeikIAQq"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r3k2r/pbpn1p1p/1p2pp2/5q2/2PP4/5N2/P1PQBPPP/R3K2R w KQkq - 1 12"]
[ChapterId "S03"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Development"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 4, move 12"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ His pieces face your king side. Where can your king find a safer home? } 12. O-O-O { Your king leaves the g column and your rook enters the game in one move. [%tail] [%cal Ge1c1] } (12. Rf1 { The rook steps across to defend the king side. It guards — but your king is still standing in the middle, and the rook on a1 is still asleep. } 12... O-O-O { He tucks his own king away while yours waits in the center. Castling long moves your king and your rook in a single move. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (12. Rg1 { You meet his rook on the g column before it even arrives. That is a defender's move, and your king is still on the wrong wing. } 12... O-O-O { His king reaches safety and yours has not moved. The g column is not the real problem — your king standing beside it is. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (12. Nh4 { The knight pokes his queen and asks her to move. She has plenty of good squares, and your king still has none. } 12... Qg5 { She simply steps to g5, still aimed at your king side. You spent the move on her instead of on your own king. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (12. Qc3 { The queen takes a central square. Usually right — but she was never the piece in danger, and your king still is. } 12... Rg8 { His rook joins the attack on the g column while your king has nowhere to go. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (12. O-O { This is the opposite plan. His queen, his rook and the bishop on b7 all point at that wing, and your king has just walked in among them. [%csl Rg1][%cal Rb7g2] } 12... Rg8 { There is the rook on the g column, and your king is sitting at the end of it. You castled into the attack he spent the whole game preparing. [%cal Rg8g1] }) 12... O-O-O 13. Qe3 { Your queen centralizes, and your rook presses the d column. Both kings ended up on the same wing, so his prepared attack has nowhere to land. [%cal Gd2e3] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Open a route for the bishop"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r5k1/pp1q1ppp/2pp4/2b2P2/4Q3/2P5/PP1R2PP/2K1R3_b_-_-_1_24"]
[White "Opponent"]
