[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Development"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 8, move 24"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ You gave up the exchange. Can your pawns help your bishop join the fight? } 24... d5 { You clear d6 for the bishop, so it can swing onto a live diagonal and back up your pawn majority. [%tail] [%cal Gd6d5] } (24... Qd8 { The queen steps back, but the bishop still needs a route into the game. } 25. Rde2 25... Qg5+ 26. Kc2 { The queen retreat costs exactly the move your bishop needed, and it is still buried behind your own pawns. [%cal Gd6d5] }) (24... h6 { You protect the king side while the bishop remains cut off. } 25. f6 25... a5 26. fxg7 { His pawn storms through to g7 while your bishop watches from c5 with nothing to do. [%cal Gd6d5] }) (24... h5 { The pawn gains space, but it does not free your bishop. } 25. f6 25... d5 26. fxg7 { Your pawn break arrives a move too late: his f pawn has already crashed through. [%cal Gd6d5] }) (24... Rd8 { Your rook steps aside and does nothing to free the bishop stuck behind your own pawns. } 25. Qe7 25... Qxe7 26. Rxe7 { Trading queens hands him the seventh row, and your bishop never got the diagonal that was one pawn move away. [%cal Gd6d5] }) 25. Qf4 25... Bd6 { Your bishop swings onto the diagonal you just opened and attacks his queen on f4. [%cal Gc5d6] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca plans: Shut the bishop out"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/ppp2p2/3b3p/2p1p1p1/4P3/3P1PBP/PPP2P2/R4RK1_b_-_-_0_15"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/Y1pH9J2T"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r4rk1/ppp2p2/3b3p/2p1p1p1/4P3/3P1PBP/PPP2P2/R4RK1 b - - 0 15"]
[ChapterId "S05"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Restrict"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 54, move 15"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ Restrict a piece. Play your pawn to f6 and keep his bishop behind its pawns. [%cal Yf7f6] } 15... f6 { Now a pawn holds e5, so that pawn never has to move again. His bishop on g3 has no way past it. [%tail] [%cal Gf7f6] } (15... a5 { You start play on the queen side. Useful in itself — but his bishop on g3 is still one pawn break away from getting out. } 16. a4 { He stops your pawn and nothing has changed for his bishop. The little move f6 is what nails your e5 pawn down for good. [%cal Gf7f6] }) (15... h5 { Space on the wing. Meanwhile your e5 pawn is held by a piece, not by a pawn, so his bishop can still hope for f4. } 16. h4 { He answers on the same wing, and the bishop still dreams of the f4 square. One pawn to f6 takes that dream away for ever. [%cal Gf7f6] }) (15... Kh7 { The king steps aside first. Nothing is wrong with it, but it is the pawns that decide whether his bishop ever breathes. } 16. Kh2 { Two kings shuffle, and his bishop is no more shut in than it was a move ago. [%cal Gf7f6] }) (15... Kg7 { The king walks toward the middle, usually the right idea. Here the bishop on g3 is the piece to settle first. } 16. Kg2 { He copies you and waits. A pawn on f6 would already have settled that bishop's fate for the rest of the game. [%cal Gf7f6] }) (15... g4 { The pawn lunges forward, but trading it opens lines for the very bishop you are trying to keep asleep. } 16. fxg4 { His f pawn takes, keeping the h pawn for an advance. } 16... Rae8 17. h4 { Now his bishop finally has the diagonal it was missing, and the whole plan to shut it out is gone. [%cal Rh3h4] }) 16. Kg2 16... b5 { Your pawn grabs queen-side space while his bishop stays boxed in behind its own pawns. [%cal Gb7b5] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca plans: Occupy the hole"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3brk1/pp2q1pp/1np1p3/2Nn1p2/3P4/3BPN2/PP3PPP/2RQ1RK1_w_-_-_2_15"]
[White "Student"]
