[FEN "r3brk1/pp2q1pp/1np1p3/2Nn1p2/3P4/3BPN2/PP3PPP/2RQ1RK1 w - - 2 15"]
[ChapterId "S06"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Restrict"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 67, move 15"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ Black has left a hole in the center. Give your knight a permanent home. } 15. Ne5 { Your knight lands on e5, and he has no pawn left that can ever attack it. That is a home for the rest of the game. [%tail] [%cal Gf3e5] } (15. Qb3 { The queen swings out to the flank. A fine square — but a queen can always be chased away, and the hole on e5 is yours for keeps. } 15... Rb8 { He tidies his rook up and nothing of his has changed. A knight planted on e5 is a gain no pawn of his can ever take back. [%cal Gf3e5] }) (15. Qc2 { The queen adds her weight to the center from c2. Perfectly good, and completely temporary — the outpost is the gain that lasts. [%cal Gf3e5] } 15... Nb4 { His knight hops to b4 and pokes at your pieces. Meanwhile e5 sits empty, still waiting for the horse that could live there. [%cal Gf3e5] }) (15. Qd2 { You join the rooks up and keep everything defended. A useful move in almost any position, and this one has something better on offer. } 15... Rd8 { His rook comes to the middle. The hole on e5 has not gone anywhere, but every move you spend elsewhere is a move he can use to cover it. [%cal Gf3e5] }) (15. Qe1 { The queen shuffles across to e1. Nothing is wrong with the square — it is the knight that has somewhere far better to be. } 15... Nc8 { He tucks the knight back and starts looking for a better home for it. The best square on the board is e5, and it is still free. [%cal Gf3e5] }) (15. Nxb7 { A free pawn is tempting. But your knight leaves the one square on the board that nothing of his can ever attack. } 15... Qxb7 16. Qd2 16... Nd7 { You swapped a knight that could never be chased for a single pawn. The outpost was worth far more than that. [%cal Rb6d7] }) 15... Rb8 16. Re1 16... Nd7 17. Ncxd7 { Your other knight takes the trade, and the knight on e5 stays exactly where it is — a permanent piece nothing can dislodge. [%cal Gc5d7] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Challenge the advanced knight"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r1k1/1b1rn1pp/ppppNp2/5P2/4P3/2N5/PPP3PP/3R1RK1_b_-_-_5_17"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
