[ChapterId "S07"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Restrict"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 5, move 17 (annotated improvement)"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ That white knight cramps your rooks. Can a pawn break give them room? } 17... c5 { You clear the c6 square first, so that when the break on d5 comes it is your bishop, not a pawn, that takes back. [%tail] [%cal Gc6c5] } (17... d5 { The right idea, one move too early. Your own pawn on c6 blocks the bishop, so after a trade on d5 it is a pawn that has to take back. } 18. Na4 { He sidesteps and holds everything together. Play c5 first, and it is the bishop on b7 that recaptures on d5 and comes alive. [%cal Gc6c5] }) (17... Kf7 { The king steps up and eyes his knight, but the pawn on f5 guards it. Your rooks are still boxed in behind your own pawns. } 18. Rf3 { He lifts the rook and keeps his grip on the position. A knight that strong is loosened by a pawn break, not by a king walk. [%cal Gc6c5] }) (17... Nc8 { The knight steps back to defend. That is one more piece crowded behind your pawns, and his knight on e6 is as comfortable as ever. } 18. g4 { He takes more space on the king side while you shuffle at home. The pawn break is the only door out of a cramped position. [%cal Gc6c5] }) (17... a5 { A pawn move on the far wing. It wins a square out there and changes nothing about the knight sitting in the middle of your camp. } 18. Rf3 { His rook joins in and your position is as tight as it was. Free the bishop with c5, then break with d5. [%cal Gc6c5] }) 18. Rf3 18... d5 19. exd5 19... Bxd5 { Your bishop recaptures and comes alive on the long diagonal — it now attacks his knight on e6 too. [%cal Gb7d5][%csl Re6] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca plans: Use the central knight"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rn2rk2/2p2p2/p2b3p/3Pp3/P6N/8/1PB2PPP/R4RK1_w_-_-_0_24"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/PdZmbIg5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rn2rk2/2p2p2/p2b3p/3Pp3/P6N/8/1PB2PPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 24"]
[ChapterId "S08"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Restrict"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 7, move 24"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ The queens are gone. Where can your knight attack while staying active? } 24. Nf5 { Your knight eyes d6 and h6. It joins the attack without another pawn move. [%tail] [%cal Gh4f5] } (24. b3 { You tidy up the queen side. Nothing wrong with it — but your knight on h4 is the worst piece on the board, and it stays there. } 24... Nd7 { He brings his last piece out. The bishop on d6 and the pawn on h6 are both loose, and f5 is the square that hits them together. [%cal Gh4f5] }) (24. Rab1 { The rook shuffles across behind its own pawn. Meanwhile your knight sits on the rim, where a knight is always worth less. } 24... Nd7 { The rook found a square and the knight did not. From f5 it would attack the bishop and the h pawn in one jump. [%cal Gh4f5] }) (24. a5 { The pawn takes another square on the queen side. A small gain — and there is a bigger one available this very move. } 24... Nd7 { He develops in peace. Your knight is still stranded on the edge with nothing to do. [%cal Gh4f5] }) (24. Rfd1 { The rook comes to the middle, a good habit in most positions. Here it is the knight, not the rook, that is out of the game. } 24... Nd7 { His knight comes out, and his loose bishop and loose pawn stay loose. A knight on f5 asks them both a question at once. [%cal Gh4f5] }) (24. Bd1 { The bishop steps back and gives up its diagonal for nothing. Your knight on h4 is still the piece crying out for a square. } 24... Nd7 25. Rc1 25... Rab8 { His rook connects, and your knight reaches f5 anyway — one move later than it could have. [%cal Gh4f5] }) 24... Nd7 25. Nxh6 { Your knight scoops up the h pawn for free — every square it eyed from f5 is now paying off. [%cal Gf5h6] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Give back the exchange"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5qk1/pb5p/3br3/2p1p2Q/2Pp1p2/1P3P2/P3R1PP/4RNK1_w_-_-_3_29"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/ShZWXTJL"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "5qk1/pb5p/3br3/2p1p2Q/2Pp1p2/1P3P2/P3R1PP/4RNK1 w - - 3 29"]
[ChapterId "S09"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Trades"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 53, move 29"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ Now let us trade. Give back material and capture the e5 pawn with your rook. [%cal Ye2e5] } 29. Rxe5 { You return the exchange to remove the center of his attack. [%cal Ge2e5] } (29. Nd2 { The knight steps in to help and keeps your extra material. Reasonable — but his pawns on d4, e5 and f4 still squeeze you. } 29... Kh8 { He tucks his king away and that pawn mass is untouched. Taking on e5 breaks it up, and the exchange is a fair price. [%cal Ge2e5] }) (29. Kh1 { Your king steps into the corner. A safe little move that does nothing at all about the pawns pressing in on you. } 29... Qf7 30. Qh3 30... Kg7 { Your king found a corner and his position is as strong as it was. Handing the exchange back on e5 is what clears the air. [%cal Ge2e5] }) (29. Qh3 { The queen shifts across to h3. Nothing of yours was hanging, and the pawn wedge in the middle is still exactly where it was. } 29... Bc8 30. Qh4 30... Bc7 { His bishop shuffles home safely. The pawn on e5 is the heart of his position, and it is still standing. [%cal Ge2e5] }) (29. Kf2 { Your king walks toward the middle — toward his pawns and both his bishops. That is the one direction it must not go. } 29... Rh6 30. Qg4+ 30... Rg6 { His rook is chased back, but your king is out in the open and his pawns have not moved. [%cal Ge2e5] }) 29... Bxe5 { His bishop takes. Use your other rook to take the bishop on e5. [%cal Ye1e5] } 30. Rxe5 { The dangerous bishop and central pawn are gone. Your rook is active. [%cal Ge1e5] } (30. Ng3 { Your knight sidesteps instead of recapturing the loose bishop on e5, so Black simply keeps the extra piece. } 30... Qf6 31. Ne4 31... Bxe4 { His other bishop grabs the misplaced knight too — recapturing on e5 right away would have avoided losing a second piece. [%cal Rb7e4] }) (30. Nd2 { Your knight retreats instead of recapturing the loose bishop on e5, so Black simply keeps the extra piece. } 30... Qf6 31. Kf1 31... Bc6 { His bishop settles safely on c6 with the extra piece banked — recapturing on e5 right away would have kept the material even. [%cal Rb7c6] }) (30. a4 { Your pawn advances on the wing instead of recapturing the loose bishop on e5, so Black simply keeps the extra piece. } 30... Qe7 31. Qg4+ 31... Kh8 { Check chases your king to the corner while Black is still up a whole piece — recapturing on e5 right away would have kept things level. [%cal Rg8h8] }) (30. Qg5+ { The check gains a tempo, but the bishop on e5 is still hanging there and Black gets to save it next. } 30... Qg7 31. Qf5 31... Re7 { His rook re-routes to safety with the extra piece banked — recapturing on e5 immediately would have kept the material even. [%cal Re6e7] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Exchange the important defender"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2rqk1nr/pp3ppp/2n1b3/2bp4/8/2N2NP1/PP2PPBP/R1BQ1RK1_w_k_-_0_10"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/ivfPvM94"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2rqk1nr/pp3ppp/2n1b3/2bp4/8/2N2NP1/PP2PPBP/R1BQ1RK1 w k - 0 10"]
[ChapterId "S10"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Trades"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 2, move 10"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ Can your knight challenge the bishop that holds his center together? } 10. Ng5 { The knight sets off for e6, to trade away the bishop that holds his center together. [%cal Gf3g5] } (10. e4 { You challenge the middle with a pawn instead. Just as good a move, and a different plan: this chapter wants that bishop off the board. } 10... Nf6 { He develops and his centre still has its guard. Removing the bishop on e6 is the gain we are after here. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (10. Qb3 { The queen eyes b7 and d5 from b3. She presses the pawn — without ever removing the piece that holds it. [%cal Gb3d5] } 10... Nf6 { His knight comes out and the bishop keeps its post. Pressure is pleasant; taking the defender off is permanent. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (10. Qd3 { The queen lines up on the d column and adds a second attacker to d5. His bishop on e6 answers that easily enough. } 10... h6 { And now his pawn takes g5 away from your knight. The trade you wanted has become much harder to arrange. [%cal Rf3g5] }) (10. a3 { A useful little pawn move on the queen side. It leaves the piece that holds his whole position together exactly where it is. } 10... Nf6 { He develops in comfort. Every quiet move you play is another move he can use to make that bishop safe. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (10. Bg5 { The bishop steps onto g5 — the very square your knight needs for the trip to e6. } 10... Nge7 { His knight steps out to e7. Your own knight still wants the g5 square, and your bishop has just taken it. [%cal Gf3g5] }) 10... Nf6 { He develops, but the bishop is still available to exchange. } 11. Nxe6 { You take the guard off his light squares. His f pawn has to recapture, and your bishop on g2 stares straight through at d5. [%cal Gg5e6][%cal Gg2d5] } (11. Bf4 { The bishop swings out and leans on c7. Active enough — but your knight came to g5 for the bishop on e6, and it is still there. } 11... O-O { He castles out of trouble, and the knight on g5 has nothing to show for the trip. [%cal Gg5e6] }) (11. Na4 { The other knight heads for c5. A decent idea, though it is the bishop on e6, not the one on c5, that holds his position up. } 11... Bb4 { His bishop sidesteps and pokes at your knight. The guard on d5 is still standing. [%cal Gg5e6] }) (11. a3 { A quiet pawn move while your knight sits on g5 with a job half done. } 11... O-O { He castles his king to safety and the trade never happened. A knight on g5 is only worth something if it jumps. [%cal Gg5e6] }) (11. Qb3 { The queen joins the diagonal toward d5 and e6. Two attackers are good; taking the defender off the board is better. [%cal Bb3e6] } 11... O-O { He tucks his king away and keeps everything guarded. Your knight still has that one job left to do. [%cal Gg5e6] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Remove the king defender"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1k1r2r1/pbpn1pqp/1p2pp2/8/2PP1P1N/3RQ1P1/P1P1B2P/2KR4_w_-_-_1_19"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
