{ His bishop guards the light squares near his king. Can you challenge it? } 19. Bf3 { Your bishop offers a trade. His king will miss that light-squared guard. [%cal Ge2f3] } (19. c5 { The pawn charges at his king's shelter, and that is strong too. This chapter takes the light-squared guard off first. } 19... f5 { His pawn steps up to slow the storm, and the bishop on b7 still covers every light square near his king. [%cal Ge2f3] }) (19. a4 { Another pawn joins the march. A real plan — it simply leaves the best defender he owns sitting on b7. } 19... Qf8 { His queen steps back to help out, and that bishop has still not been challenged. [%cal Ge2f3] }) (19. Nf3 { The knight takes f3, the square your own bishop needed. Now you cannot even offer the trade of light-squared bishops. } 19... Rde8 20. c5 20... e5 { His pawn breaks in the middle while your pieces stand in each other's way. [%cal Ge2f3] }) (19. Bh5 { The bishop goes the other way, where it eyes nothing in particular. The trade you wanted was one square in the other direction. } 19... Ba6 20. Bf3 20... f5 { He gets a free developing move out of your detour, and the bishops are still both on the board. [%cal Ge2f3] }) 19... Rge8 { His rook shifts across the back row. Now go and finish the exchange you set up last move. } 20. Bxb7 { His light-squared defender is gone. Your c pawn can help open the attack. [%cal Gf3b7] } (20. c5 { Stronger, even: the pawn goes straight for his king. This chapter takes the bishop off first, so those light squares stay weak for ever. } 20... c6 { His pawn blocks the diagonal and the trade is gone for good. Taking on b7 a move earlier could not have been stopped. [%cal Gf3b7] }) (20. a4 { The pawn marches on the same wing, one move slower. The bishop is still there, still guarding his king. } 20... Qf8 { His queen shuffles about, and his bishop is still minding the squares around his king. [%cal Gf3b7] }) (20. Ra3 { The rook swings across to join the attack. A good idea that costs a move — and he can spend that move on c6, shutting the diagonal. } 20... c5 { He takes space of his own and the bishop stays. Trading it off is the one gain he can never undo. [%cal Gf3b7] }) (20. Rb3 { The rook aims down the b column at his king, where his own bishop and pawn are in the way. The trade on b7 clears them. } 20... Qf8 21. c5 21... e5 { His pawn breaks in the middle, and the defender of his king is still on the board. [%cal Gf3b7] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Trade queens without losing activity"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5rk1/1b2bppp/p4n2/1p1p2B1/2q5/2N1PN2/PP2QPPP/3R2K1_w_-_-_2_18"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/ig7lP9ZA"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "5rk1/1b2bppp/p4n2/1p1p2B1/2q5/2N1PN2/PP2QPPP/3R2K1 w - - 2 18"]
[ChapterId "S12"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Trades"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 9, move 18"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ The queens can come off. Keep your knights working together. } 18. Nd4 { You centralize first, leaving the other knight ready to take the queen. [%cal Gf3d4] } (18. a3 { A tidy pawn move that takes b4 from him. Nothing wrong with it — but the queens are about to come off, and your knights have not agreed who takes back. } 18... h6 { He makes a little air for his king. When the trade comes, your knights will still be arguing over e2. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (18. Nd2 { This knight heads for the middle by the other road. From d2 it gets in its own rook's way, and d4 stays empty. } 18... Qxe2 { He trades at once, and now the recapture leaves a knight on d2 and nobody holding d4. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (18. Ne5 { The knight jumps to an outpost and looks impressive. He can trade queens whenever he likes, and your knights are still out of step. } 18... Qc7 { His queen steps aside and keeps the tension. Meanwhile d4 is the square your two knights were meant to hold between them. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (18. Qd2 { You offer the trade yourself. Fair enough — but he chooses when to take, and your knights are not ready for it yet. } 18... b4 { His pawn pushes on and your knight on c3 has to start worrying. Settling d4 first keeps everything together. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (18. Rd4 { The rook attacks the queen, and it takes for itself the square your knight was heading to. } 18... Qxe2 { He trades anyway, and a rook on d4 is not what keeps your knights working as a pair. [%cal Gf3d4] }) 18... Qxe2 { He trades queens too soon. Which knight keeps your pieces connected? } 19. Ncxe2 { One knight holds d4 and the other supports it. Your pieces stay connected. [%cal Gc3e2] } (19. Ndxe2 { The wrong knight. This one leaves d4, the very square the pair was working to hold. } 19... h6 20. Bh4 20... b4 { His pawn advances, and recapturing with the other knight would have kept d4 covered. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (19. Nf5 { Your knight jumps forward and ignores the queen sitting on e2, and she is not done yet — she grabs your rook with check next. } 19... Qxd1+ 20. Nxd1 20... Re8 { Your knight scoops the queen back, but his rook develops for free — you are down a whole rook for letting her play on. [%cal Rf8e8] }) (19. e4 { This pawn push ignores the queen sitting on e2, and she gets to grab a piece before you ever collect her. } 19... Qg4 20. Nf5 20... Qxg5 { His queen takes your bishop, not just the e pawn. Your attack has cost a piece. [%cal Rg4g5] }) (19. Bxf6 { This trade ignores the queen sitting on e2, and she is not done yet — she grabs your rook with check next. } 19... Qxd1+ 20. Nxd1 20... Bxf6 { He recaptures the bishop after your knight scoops the queen back — you are down a whole rook for letting her play on. [%cal Re7f6] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Break against the fixed pawn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r1k1/pp1bqnpp/2p1pr2/4Np2/2PP4/3B3Q/PP2RPPP/4R1K1_w_-_-_1_19"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/DqfMomWg"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "4r1k1/pp1bqnpp/2p1pr2/4Np2/2PP4/3B3Q/PP2RPPP/4R1K1 w - - 1 19"]
[ChapterId "S13"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Pawns"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 52, move 19"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ A central pawn break. Push to d5 and attack the pawn holding his pieces back. [%cal Yd4d5] } 19. d5 { You challenge e6 while your rooks and bishop are ready to use the open lines. [%cal Gd4d5] } (19. c5 { Every bit as strong, maybe stronger: you clamp the queen side and leave his bishop bad. This chapter opens the middle instead. } 19... Rh6 { His rook swings across at your queen, and the pawn on e6 is as comfortable as ever. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (19. Nxf7 { A sharp alternative and every bit as good: the knight grabs f7 and invites his rook to take it. } 19... Rxf7 { He takes back and a scramble follows that turns out well for you. Today we are learning the quiet pawn break, not the trick. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (19. Rd2 { The rook lines up behind the d pawn, ready for the break. Preparing is sound — but the break works this very move. } 19... Rd8 { His rook comes to meet yours, and he has had a free move to look after e6. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (19. Qe3 { The queen returns to the middle. Solid and useful, and it leaves his backward pawn exactly as safe as it was. } 19... Rd8 { He puts a rook on the column you were about to open. Break first and he never gets the chance. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (19. Qg3 { The queen looks toward his king. A fair idea — but the pawn on e6 is the thing that cannot move, and that is where the game is. } 19... Bc8 { His bishop goes home to mind e6, the very defence your pawn break was meant to prevent. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (19. Nxd7 { You swap your monster knight for his bishop. That is the trade he has been hoping for: your best piece for his worst. } 19... Qxd7 20. Qe3 20... f4 { This leaves you worse off. His pawn advances instead, and giving up the strong knight was not worth it. [%cal Rf5f4] }) 19... Nxe5 { Taking on d5 would invite your bishop to c4, so he trades the knights instead. Take back with the rook. [%cal Ye2e5] } 20. Rxe5 { Your rook stays active on e5. His e6 pawn still ties down his pieces. [%cal Ge2e5] } (20. d6 { Pushing the pawn lets his queen take it back for free. } 20... Qxd6 21. Rxe5 21... c5 { His pawn advances on the queen side, and recapturing on e5 right away kept more control. [%cal Rc6c5] }) (20. dxe6 { This capture leaves the knight on e5 completely unaddressed, and the e file opens for him too. } 20... Rxe6 21. Bxf5 21... Rh6 { His rook swings to h6 and hits your queen on h3, and the knight on e5 was never even dealt with. [%cal Re6h6][%cal Rh6h3] }) (20. Bc2 { Your bishop retreats and leaves the knight on e5 completely unaddressed. } 20... Ng6 21. dxe6 21... Nf4 { His knight jumps to f4, forking your queen on h3 and your rook on e2 — recapturing on e5 immediately would have avoided the whole disaster. [%cal Rf4h3][%cal Rf4e2] }) (20. Bb1 { Your bishop tucks into the corner and leaves the knight on e5 completely unaddressed. } 20... Ng6 21. dxe6 21... Nf4 { His knight lands on f4 again, forking your queen and rook the same way — the rook recapture on e5 was still the tidier answer. [%cal Rf4h3][%cal Rf4e2] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Mobilize the king-side majority"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2r1k2/2p2p2/p2b4/4pN2/P1B2n2/8/1P3PPP/R2R2K1_w_-_-_4_30"]
[White "Student"]
