[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Pawns"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 7, move 30"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ You have more king-side pawns. Can you start putting that majority to work? } 30. h4 { Your h pawn takes space and starts the king-side majority marching forward. [%tail] [%cal Gh2h4] } (30. g3 { The same plan with the other pawn first. It works just as well — a majority only needs one pawn to start it. [%cal Gh2h4] } 30... Ne6 { Either order rolls the pawns forward. The h pawn is the one with the longest road, so it usually leads. [%cal Gh2h4] }) (30. b3 { The pawn tidies the queen side. You keep every bit of your advantage — you simply have not moved the three pawns that will win the game. } 30... Bc5 { His bishop finds a new post while your three against one on the king side has not moved a square. [%cal Gh2h4] }) (30. Rac1 { The rook swings across and leans on his c pawn. Useful — but the pawns that decide this game are on the other wing. } 30... Bc5 { His bishop shifts across, and your king-side pawns still stand exactly where they began. [%cal Gh2h4] }) (30. Kf1 { The king starts walking toward the middle, right in most endings. Here the pawns go first and the king follows them. } 30... a5 { He takes queen-side space while your majority waits. Start it rolling and his lone f pawn cannot hold three. [%cal Gh2h4] }) 30... Ne6 31. g3 { Your pawn joins the push: three pawns against his one, and the h pawn has no enemy pawn on its own column or on either side of it. [%cal Gg2g3] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Open the exposed king position"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3n4/ppq2pp1/2p2k2/4p1p1/1PP1Q3/4PN1P/P4PP1/6K1_w_-_-_0_24"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
