[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Pawns"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 3, move 24"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ His king must help defend the pawns. Can you give your queen a way in? } 24. h4 { Your h pawn challenges g5. A capture opens the h column for your queen. [%tail] [%cal Gh3h4] } (24. c5 { The pawn takes space on the far side of the board. His king is not over there — it is stuck minding the pawns beside it. } 24... g6 25. h4 25... gxh4 { The break on h4 arrives a move later, and that move was exactly what his king needed. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (24. Qg4 { The queen steps up to eye the pawn on g5. His king guards it, and the column she wants is still closed. [%csl Rg5] } 24... Ne6 25. h4 25... gxh4 { You spent a move and he did not. Open the column with the pawn first; the queen comes in behind it. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (24. a3 { A little pawn move on the quiet wing, far from the king you are hunting. } 24... g6 25. h4 25... gxh4 { He gets a free move to arrange his defence before the break lands. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (24. Qh7 { The queen dives in on her own. There is no open road yet, and one piece will never break a king that still has pawns around it. } 24... Ne6 25. h4 25... g4 { This time he leaves the pawns alone, and the column never opens at all. [%cal Gh3h4] }) 24... gxh4 25. Qxh4+ { Your queen recaptures on the newly opened h column, and it comes with check — his king has nowhere safe to hide. [%cal Ge4h4] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Stop the freeing pawn advance"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r1k1/pb3ppp/1pN5/4P3/8/2R5/PP1r1PPP/1R4K1_b_-_-_0_22"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
