[ChapterId "S16"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Pawns"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 14, move 22"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ White would like to play f4 and free his pieces. Can a pawn stop it? } 22... g5 { Your g pawn takes f4 away. The pressure on e5 keeps his knight tied down. [%tail] [%cal Gg7g5] } (22... g6 { You prop the pawn up instead of pushing it. Solid — and f4 is still a move he can play whenever he likes. } 23. a3 { He makes a quiet move because he is allowed to. A pawn on g5 takes f4 away from him for good. [%cal Gg7g5] }) (22... Bxc6 { You trade your bishop for his knight. An even swap, and his freeing pawn break is still sitting there waiting. [%csl Rf4] } 23. Rxc6 { His rook takes back, and now nothing at all stops f4. [%cal Gg7g5] }) (22... h6 { A quiet move for your king's comfort. It does nothing about f4, the one move his whole position is waiting for. } 23. a3 { He improves quietly, and the break that frees his pieces is still there for the taking. [%cal Gg7g5] }) (22... h5 { The pawn takes space on the wing. It leaves f4 untouched, and f4 is the square this position turns on. } 23. a4 { He answers on the far wing, holding f4 in reserve. One pawn to g5 would have taken it off the table. [%cal Gg7g5] }) (22... Rxe5 { That pawn is defended by the knight. Taking it loses a rook. } 23. Nxe5 23... g6 24. Nf3 { Giving up the rook for a pawn was never the way to stop f4 — g5 does that for free. [%cal Re5f3] }) 23. h3 23... Bxc6 24. Rxc6 24... Rxe5 { Your rook collects the e5 pawn now that nothing defends it — the pressure you kept up finally cashes in. [%cal Ge8e5] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca plans: Lift the rook before doubling"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2kr2r1/pbpn1p1p/1p2pp2/q7/2PP4/4QNP1/P1P1BP1P/2KR3R_w_-_-_1_15"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
