[ChapterId "S17"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Rooks"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 4, move 15"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ A rook lift. Move your rook to d3 so the other rook can join it on d1. [%cal Yd1d3] } 15. Rd3 { Your rook leaves room on d1. The pair can challenge the center together. [%cal Gd1d3] } (15. Kb2 { The king shuffles across to b2. A safe little move, and your two rooks are still no closer to working together. } 15... c5 { His pawn strikes at your center while your rooks sit apart. The lift to d3 is what joins them up. [%cal Gd1d3] }) (15. a3 { A pawn makes some air for your king. Nothing wrong with it, except that the rook in the corner is still out of the game. } 15... c5 { He matches you on the queen side, and your second rook has not moved an inch. [%cal Gd1d3] }) (15. Qb3 { The queen leaves d4 to look after itself, and she leaves one move too early. } 15... e5 16. Rhe1 16... exd4 { His pawn takes the center, and the rook lift would have kept d4 properly defended. [%cal Gd1d3] }) (15. Kb1 { The king takes another step sideways. It costs nothing but a move — and a move is exactly what the rook lift needed. } 15... f5 16. Rd3 16... Nf6 { His knight develops while you shuffle, and your lift arrives a move late. [%cal Gd1d3] }) (15. Nh4 { Your knight heads to h4 and abandons f3, opening the whole diagonal to your own rook in the corner. } 15... Bxh1 { His bishop takes a rook, not just a pawn. Your knight left the diagonal open. } 16. Rxh1 16... Qxa2 { His queen grabs a second pawn, and the rook lift never even got the chance to matter. [%cal Ra5a2] }) 15... Kb8 { He spends a move tucking his king away. Now bring the rook in the corner to d1. [%cal Yh1d1] } 16. Rhd1 { Both rooks face the center. Your lift made room without blocking the queen. [%cal Gh1d1] } (16. Ra3 { The lifted rook changes wings and eyes a7. Tempting — but it gives up the doubling you spent last move preparing. } 16... Qb4 { His queen jumps forward, and you have one rook here and one still in the corner. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (16. Re1 { This rook takes e1 instead of joining its partner. A fair square, and the d column gets only half the pressure. } 16... e5 { His pawn claims the center, and your second rook is one square away from where it belonged. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (16. a3 { A quiet pawn move. The rook in the corner has now been waiting two moves for its turn. } 16... e5 17. dxe5 17... Nc5 { His knight lands on a fine square, and doubling the rooks first would have kept him quiet. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (16. Kb2 { Another king step. Safe, and it leaves the rook in the corner exactly where it was. } 16... e5 17. Rhd1 17... e4 { His pawn pushes on, and the rooks could have been doubled two moves ago. [%cal Gh1d1] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Transfer the rook across"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r1k1/pb3p1p/1pN5/4P3/7p/2R5/PP1r1PP1/4R1K1_b_-_-_1_24"]
[White "Opponent"]
