[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Rooks"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part II, Game 14, move 24"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ Your first rook is already active. Find a route across for the second one. } 24... Re6 { The sixth rank is your bridge to g6. Both rooks can attack the king side. [%tail] [%cal Ge8e6] } (24... Rxb2 { You collect a pawn. A pawn is a pawn — but it sends your second rook away from the attack, and the attack is worth far more here. } 25. Nd4 { His knight holds the middle and his king breathes again. Your two rooks needed to arrive together. [%cal Ge8e6] }) (24... a5 { A pawn move on the far wing while your second rook is still sitting at home. } 25. b4 { He answers on the queen side, and your rook is still looking for a road across. The sixth row is that road. [%cal Ge8e6] }) (24... a6 { A quiet pawn move that changes nothing at all. The rook on e8 is the piece with somewhere to be. } 25. Re4 { His rook comes out to defend, and your second rook has not even started its journey. [%cal Ge8e6] }) (24... h3 { The pawn lunges at his king's cover on its own. He simply takes it, and your rook is no nearer the attack. } 25. gxh3 { He grabs the pawn and his king is fine. One rook alone is not an attack. [%cal Ge8e6] }) 25. Rec1 25... Kg7 26. b4 26... b5 27. Kf1 27... Rg6 { Your rook completes the bridge, landing on g6 — both rooks now bear down on his king side. [%cal Ge6g6] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca plans: Choose activity over a recapture"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3r4/p4ppk/5b1p/2pR1P2/q5P1/P7/1P4QP/1K1R4_b_-_-_0_38"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/eP0XZTt6/cKdnpmDm"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "3r4/p4ppk/5b1p/2pR1P2/q5P1/P7/1P4QP/1K1R4 b - - 0 38"]
[ChapterId "S19"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Rooks"]
