{ His rook has taken a pawn. Can your own rook create a stronger threat? } 38... Rb8 { Your rook takes the open b column. His king, not the lost pawn, is the target. [%tail] [%cal Gd8b8] } (38... Re8 { Your rook stays passive on the e column instead of grabbing the open one. } 39. h4 39... Bxh4 40. Rd7 { His rook reaches d7. The direct b-column attack would have kept him defending. [%cal Rd5d7] }) (38... Rc8 { Your rook doubles on a column that his king does not live on. The open one next door is the road in. } 39. h4 39... Rb8 { Your rook targets the king instead of collecting the h pawn. } 40. R1d2 { He gets time to organize while your rooks press a column that leads nowhere. [%cal Gd8b8] }) (38... Qc6 { Your queen retreats to c6, guarding the c5 pawn but giving up the initiative completely. [%csl Bc5] } 39. g5 39... Bxg5 40. f6 { His f pawn rolls on. Your queen is tied to one pawn, and his king can breathe again. [%cal Gd8b8] }) (38... Rf8 { Your rook heads toward f8, away from the column that actually presses his king. } 39. g5 39... hxg5 40. Qc2 { He has time to sort his defense out, and your rook is on the wrong side of the board. [%cal Gd8b8] }) (38... Qxd1+ { You hand over the queen for both rooks. The material is fair, and the attack that was winning disappears with her. } 39. Rxd1 39... Rxd1+ 40. Kc2 { Queens off, and the threats around his king are gone. Keeping her is what made the b column deadly. [%cal Gd8b8] }) 39. R1d2 39... c4 { Your pawn advances again. Queen and rook both bear down on his king's wing — the pawn he grabbed on d5 is the least of your worries now. [%cal Gc5c4] } 0-1


