{ You are a pawn up. How can your rook prepare to challenge the open column? } 28. Rd2 { The rook steps off the d column so it can swing to c2 next move and claim the open one. [%cal Gd1d2] } (28. g4 { Also good: your pawns take space and shut his bishop out. This chapter goes after the open column instead. } 28... Rh8 { His rook goes hunting your h pawn, and neither side has claimed the open column yet. [%cal Gd1d2] }) (28. b3 { The pawn steps up beside c4. Safe — but it is the rook, not a pawn, that decides who owns the open column. } 28... Rc2 { And there he is on c2 first. The column belongs to whoever gets there, and he did. [%cal Gd1d2] }) (28. Rf1 { The rook shuffles to a column where nothing is happening, while the open one waits for whoever wants it. } 28... b4 29. g4 29... f5 { His pawn gains ground on the king side, and your rook is still nowhere near c2. [%cal Gd1d2] }) (28. a3 { A pawn move on the edge of the board. The rook is the piece with a job to do here. } 28... Rc2 29. b4 29... Rc3 { His rook walks into c3, because yours never came to meet it. [%cal Gd1d2] }) (28. g3 { The pawn props up h4 and hands him the time he needs to free his bishop and take the column first. } 28... f5 29. Rf1 29... Rc4 { His rook claims the c column, since yours was never sent to meet it. [%cal Gd1d2] }) 28... Rh8 { His rook goes after the h pawn. Can you seize the open column? } 29. Rc2 { You take the c column. A rook trade leaves your extra pawn and active pieces. [%cal Gd2c2] } (29. g3 { Also good: the little pawn defends h4 so his rook cannot come. The open column is still sitting there, though. [%csl Bh4] } 29... f5 { His pawn advances, and the open column is still waiting for a rook — yours or his. [%cal Gd2c2] }) (29. Bg3 { The bishop steps back to hold h4. Useful enough, and it is the rook move that decides this position. } 29... a5 { He takes a little space on the wing, and neither rook has taken the column yet. [%cal Gd2c2] }) (29. b3 { Another pawn move, and this one lets him take your h pawn before you ever reach the column. } 29... Rxh4 30. Rc2 30... d4 { His pawn advances in the center with the extra pawn back in his pocket. The column was there a move ago. [%cal Gd2c2] }) (29. a3 { The same problem again: a pawn move that hands back the pawn you were a move from keeping. } 29... Rxh4 30. Rc2 30... d4 { His pawn pushes on, and the column you wanted was free one move earlier. [%cal Gd2c2] }) 1-0
